Q: Is the E.T. the Extra-Terrestrial Atari Landfill story an urban legend?
A: The answer (Yes or No) isA multi-part answer to a multi-faceted question!

Yes, and also No!

The E.T. the Extra-Terrestrial Atari Landfill story, often referred to as the "E.T. video game burial," has been largely debunked as an urban legend. While it is true that Atari did bury a large number of unsold cartridges of the E.T. game, the specifics of the event have been exaggerated over time.

The original story claimed that Atari buried millions of unsold cartridges in a New Mexico landfill, but this has been disputed by Atari itself, which said the burial was much smaller in scale. An investigation by the BBC in 2014 even found the alleged "E.T. graveyard" was actually just a regular landfill with some old Atari boxes and a few E.T. cartridges among the trash.

However, the E.T. video game burial has become a fascinating urban legend, symbolizing the challenges of the video game industry in the early days and the risks of producing a product that fails to meet expectations. So, while the specifics of the story may be an urban legend, the cultural impact and the story's staying power are very real!

