In the United Kingdom, the diplomatic immunity of the US Secretary of State is protected by the Diplomatic Privileges Act 1964, which is a UK law that grants immunity to certain foreign officials, including ambassadors, high commissioners, and their families.

However, this immunity only applies to acts performed in the exercise of their official functions. If the Secretary of State commits a crime in the UK outside of their official duties, they may not be immune from prosecution.

That being said, even if they commit a crime outside of their official duties, the UK government might still be reluctant to arrest and prosecute a high-ranking US official, as this could strain diplomatic relations between the two countries.

